<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis VIII of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1187–1226</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Louis the Lion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis VIII, called the Lion, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1223 to 1226 and son of Philip II Augustus. As prince he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1216 at the invitation of rebel barons but was defeated at Lincoln in 1217. As king he completed the conquest of Poitou from the English in 1224 and led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Albigensians</a>. He began the practice of granting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> to royal sons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-viii-of-france.jpg" alt="Louis VIII of France" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Natalis de Wailly · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
